<p align=3D"center"> <em><strong>Examples</strong></em> </p>
<p>We first start with two examples.</p>
<p><strong><em>Example 1</em></strong><br>
Solve the following system of linear congruence equations.</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+3+%5C+%28%5Ctext=
%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201=
002" alt=3D"x \equiv 3 \ (\text{mod} \ 17)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+10+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+21%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 10 \ (\text{mod} \ 21)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+15+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+29%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 15 \ (\text{mod} \ 29)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>Both 17 and 29 are prime. The factors of 21 are 3 and 7. The three modul=
i are pairwise relatively prime (i.e. no two of which have a common factor =
other than 1). Thus CRT implies that the three equations have a solution <i=
mg src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_0&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D=
333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_0" class=3D"latex">. The soluti=
on is unique modulo 10353 =3D 17 x 21 x 29. This means that if <img src=3D"=
https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dy&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"y" class=3D"latex"> is another solution, then=
 <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dy%5Cequiv+x_0+%5C+%28%5Cte=
xt%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D=
20201002" alt=3D"y\equiv x_0 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353)" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p>The solution is <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_0%3D3+=
%5Ccdot+609+%5Ccdot+11%2B10+%5Ccdot+493+%5Ccdot+19%2B15+%5Ccdot+357+%5Ccdot=
+13+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&am=
p;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_0=3D3 \cdot 609 \cdot 11+10 \cdot 493 \c=
dot 19+15 \cdot 357 \cdot 13 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353)" class=3D"latex"> where=
</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D609%3D21+%5Ccdot+29&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"609=3D21 \cdot=
 29" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D6=
09+%5Ccdot+11+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&=
amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"609 \cdot 11 \equiv 1 \ =
(\text{mod} \ 17)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D493%3D17+%5Ccdot+29&amp;=
bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"493=3D17 \cd=
ot 29" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=
=3D493+%5Ccdot+19+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+21%29&amp;bg=3Dfff=
fff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"493 \cdot 19 \equiv =
1 \ (\text{mod} \ 21)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D357%3D17+%5Ccdot+21&amp;=
bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"357=3D17 \cd=
ot 21" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=
=3D357+%5Ccdot+13+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+29%29&amp;bg=3Dfff=
fff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"357 \cdot 13 \equiv =
1 \ (\text{mod} \ 29)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>The number 609 is the product of the remaining moduli (all the modulo no=
t 17). Because 609 and the modulus 17 are relatively prime, the number 609 =
has a multiplicative inverse modulo 17. The multiplicative inverse is 11, f=
ound using the extended Euclidean algorithm. The other two pairs =E2=80=9C4=
93 and 19=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9C357 and 13=E2=80=9D are found similarly. Th=
us the solution <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_0&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_0" class=3D"=
latex"> is the sum of three numbers. The first is 3 x 609 x 11, where 3 is =
the solution to the first equation, and 609 and 11 multiplicative inverses =
to each other modulo 17. The other two numbers 10 x 493 x 19 and 15 x 357 x=
 13 are obtained similarly.</p>
<p>After reducing modulo 10353, the solution is</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin%7B=
aligned%7D+x_0%26%5Cequiv+3+%5Ccdot+609+%5Ccdot+11%2B10+%5Ccdot+493+%5Ccdot=
+19%2B15+%5Ccdot+357+%5Ccdot+13+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29+%5C%5C=
%26%5Cequiv+20097%2B93670%2B69615+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29+%5C%=
5C%26%5Cequiv+9744%2B493%2B7497+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29+%5C%5C=
%26%5Cequiv+17734+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29+%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+73=
81+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+10353%29++%5Cend%7Baligned%7D&amp;bg=3Dfffff=
f&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\displaystyle \begin{a=
ligned} x_0&amp;\equiv 3 \cdot 609 \cdot 11+10 \cdot 493 \cdot 19+15 \cdot =
357 \cdot 13 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353) \\&amp;\equiv 20097+93670+69615 \ (\tex=
t{mod} \ 10353) \\&amp;\equiv 9744+493+7497 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353) \\&amp;\=
equiv 17734 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353) \\&amp;\equiv 7381 \ (\text{mod} \ 10353=
)  \end{aligned}" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
</ul>
<p>The algorithm demonstrated here requires the computation of three multip=
licative inverses. One try and true method for finding multiplicative inver=
se in the extended Euclidean algorithm (i.e. applying the Euclidean algorit=
hm and then work backward). We demonstrate how to solve <img src=3D"https:/=
/s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D609y+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17=
%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"609y=
 \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod} \ 17)" class=3D"latex">. To this end, solve the lin=
ear diophantine equation <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D60=
9y%2B17z%3D1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" al=
t=3D"609y+17z=3D1" class=3D"latex">. Then work backward.</p>
<ul>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin=
%7Baligned%7D+%26609%3D17+%5Ccdot+35%2B1+%5C%5C%2617%3D14+%5Ccdot+1%2B3+%5C=
%5C%2614%3D3+%5Ccdot+4%2B2+%5C%5C%263%3D2+%5Ccdot+1%2B1+%5C%5C%262%3D1+%5Cc=
dot+2%2B0+%5C%5C%26%5Ctext%7B+%7D+%5C%5C%26%5Ctext%7B+%7D+%5Cend%7Baligned%=
7D++++++++%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+%5C+++%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin%7B=
aligned%7D+1%26%3D3-2+%5C%5C%26%3D3-%2814-3+%5Ccdot+4%29+%5C%5C%26%3D14%28-=
1%29%2B3%285%29+%5C%5C%26%3D14%28-1%29%2B%2817-14%295+%5C%5C%26%3D17%285%29=
%2B14%28-6%29+%5C%5C%26%3D17%285%29%2B%28609-17+%5Ccdot+35%29+%28-6%29+%5C%=
5C%26%3D609%28-6%29%2B17%28215%29+%5Cend%7Baligned%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;f=
g=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\displaystyle \begin{aligned}=
 &amp;609=3D17 \cdot 35+1 \\&amp;17=3D14 \cdot 1+3 \\&amp;14=3D3 \cdot 4+2 =
\\&amp;3=3D2 \cdot 1+1 \\&amp;2=3D1 \cdot 2+0 \\&amp;\text{ } \\&amp;\text{=
 } \end{aligned}        \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \   \displaystyle \begin{aligned} 1=
&amp;=3D3-2 \\&amp;=3D3-(14-3 \cdot 4) \\&amp;=3D14(-1)+3(5) \\&amp;=3D14(-=
1)+(17-14)5 \\&amp;=3D17(5)+14(-6) \\&amp;=3D17(5)+(609-17 \cdot 35) (-6) \=
\&amp;=3D609(-6)+17(215) \end{aligned}" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>The calculation on the left shows the repeated applications of divisions=
 to show that <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%28609%2C17%2=
9%3D1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"(6=
09,17)=3D1" class=3D"latex">. The calculation on the right works backward t=
o solve <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D609y%2B17z%3D1&amp;=
bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"609y+17z=3D1=
" class=3D"latex">. The solution is <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php=
?latex=3Dy%3D-6&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002"=
 alt=3D"y=3D-6" class=3D"latex">, the least residue of which is <img src=3D=
"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dy%3D11&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333=
&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"y=3D11" class=3D"latex">. <img src=3D"h=
ttps://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Csquare&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D33333=
3&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\square" class=3D"latex"></p>
<div class=3D"wordads-ad-wrapper" style=3D"display: inherit;"><div id=3D"at=
atags-26942-780050"></div></div><p><strong><em>Example 2</em></strong><br>
Solve the following system of linear congruence equations.</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D3+%5C+x+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%=
5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+5%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D=
20201002" alt=3D"3 \ x \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod} \ 5)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D4+%5C+x+%5Cequiv+6+%5C+%=
28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+14%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;=
c=3D20201002" alt=3D"4 \ x \equiv 6 \ (\text{mod} \ 14)" class=3D"latex"></=
p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D5+%5C+x+%5Cequiv+11+%5C+=
%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;=
c=3D20201002" alt=3D"5 \ x \equiv 11 \ (\text{mod} \ 3)" class=3D"latex"></=
p>
</ul>
<p>The algorithm used in Example 1 works here. The only difference is that =
each equation needs to be solved individually. The numbers here are small. =
The equations can be solved by inspection. Otherwise, each equation can als=
o be solved by solving an appropriate linear diophantine equation (as discu=
ssed <a href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/2013/08/18/solv=
ing-linear-diophantine-equations/" target=3D"_blank">here</a>). The followi=
ng are the solutions to the above equations (individually).</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+2+%5C+%28%5Ctext=
%7Bmod%7D+%5C+5%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D202010=
02" alt=3D"x \equiv 2 \ (\text{mod} \ 5)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+5+%5C+%28%5Ct=
